      "id": "physics",
      "title": "Rotate-and-roll algorithm",
      "column": 2,
      "row": 0,
      "status": "done",
      "seconds": 57,
      "outputType": "markdown",
      "output": "# Paper Path: rotate-and-roll functions and solution replay\n\n**Execution status:** I could not run this code here. The positions in the table below come from tracing the levels by hand cell by cell. They are not output from a run. Calling `checkSolutions(LEVELS)` in a browser console or in Node is still pending.\n\n## Algorithm (25 lines)\n\n```js\nfunction rotate(grid, dir) {\n  const n = grid.length;\n  return grid.map((_, r) => grid.map((_, c) => dir === 'E' ? grid[n - 1 - c][r] : grid[c][n - 1 - r]).join(''));\n}\nfunction roll(grid, pos) {\n  let [r, c] = pos, win = false;\n  while (!win && grid[r + 1][c] !== '#') { r++; win = grid[r][c] === 'H'; }\n  const out = grid.map((row, i) => [...row].map((ch, j) => (i === r && j === c ? 'O' : ch === 'O' ? '.' : ch)).join(''));\n  return { grid: out, pos: [r, c], win };\n}\nfunction toBoard([r, c], k, n) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) [r, c] = [n - 1 - c, r];\n  return [r, c];\n}\nfunction replay(level, moves) {\n  let g = level.map(row => row.replace('S', 'O')), k = 0;\n  const steps = [];\n  for (const m of moves) {\n    g = rotate(g, m); k = (k + (m === 'E' ? 1 : 3)) % 4;\n    const r0 = g.findIndex(row => row.includes('O'));\n    const res = roll(g, [r0, g[r0].indexOf('O')]); g = res.grid;\n    steps.push({ move: m, k, pos: toBoard(res.pos, k, g.length), win: res.win });\n    if (res.win) break;\n  }\n  return steps;\n}\n```\n\n### What each function does\n\n- **`rotate(grid, dir)`** is pure and returns a new array of strings.\n  - `'E'` turns the grid clockwise: `new[r][c] = old[n-1-c][r]`.\n  - Anything else (`'Q'`) turns it counter-clockwise: `new[r][c] = old[c][n-1-r]`.\n  - The marble `O` and home `H` are characters in the grid, so they turn with it.\n  - Mapping the keys to a direction is the caller's job: E, → and ⟳ give `'E'`; Q, ← and ⟲ give `'Q'`.\n- **`roll(grid, pos)`** is pure. It moves the marble one cell at a time toward +row, which is \"down the screen\" in the rotated frame.\n  - It stops before the next `#` cell.\n  - It reports `win: true` the moment the marble enters `H`, whether passing over it or stopping on it.\n  - It returns a new grid with the marble moved.\n- **`toBoard(pos, k, n)`** converts a position in the rotated frame back to the original board coordinates (row, col) by applying the inverse of k clockwise quarter turns. With these mappings, gravity for k = 0, 1, 2, 3 is +row, +col, −row and −col, which matches the rules.\n- **`replay(level, moves)`** is called by the caller and nothing runs at the top level.\n  - It turns, then rolls, then records `{ move, k, pos, win }` for each step.\n  - It stops at the first win.\n  - It accepts `S` from the rules or `O` from the level format as the marble.\n\n## Checks (9 lines)\n\n```js\nfunction checkSolutions(levels) {\n  const sols = [['E','E'], ['E','E','E'], ['E','E','E','E']];\n  return levels.map((lv, i) => {\n    const steps = replay(lv, sols[i]);\n    const last = steps[steps.length - 1];\n    console.log(`Level ${i + 1}: ` + steps.map(s => `${s.move}→(${s.pos})${s.win ? ' WIN' : ''}`).join(', '));\n    return last.win && steps.length === sols[i].length;\n  }).every(Boolean);\n}\n```\n\n## Level data (copied from the \"Three levels\" input)\n\n```js\nconst LEVELS = [\n  ['#########','#......H#','#.......#','#..#....#','#....#..#','#.......#','#.......#','#O......#','#########'],\n  ['#########','#.......#','#..#....#','#......##','#.H.....#','#...#...#','#.......#','#O......#','#########'],\n  ['#########','#..#....#','#.#.....#','#.......#','#.....#.#','#...H...#','#..#....#','#O......#','#########'],\n];\n// Caller: checkSolutions(LEVELS)  → expected true\n```\n\n## Marble position after each step (hand-traced, board coordinates (row,col))\n\n| Level | Step | Move | k | Gravity | Marble ends at | Win |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1 | E | 1 | +col | (7,7) | – |\n| 1 | 2 | E | 2 | −row | (1,7) = H | **WIN** |\n| 2 | 1 | E | 1 | +col | (7,7) | – |\n| 2 | 2 | E | 2 | −row | (4,7), below the wall at (3,7) | – |\n| 2 | 3 | E | 3 | −col | (4,2) = H | **WIN** |\n| 3 | 1 | E | 1 | +col | (7,7) | – |\n| 3 | 2 | E | 2 | −row | (1,7), with column 7 clear from row 6 to row 1 | – |\n| 3 | 3 | E | 3 | −col | (1,4), beside the wall at (1,3) | – |\n| 3 | 4 | E | 0 | +row | (5,4) = H | **WIN** |\n\nExpected console output:\n\n```\nLevel 1: E→(7,7), E→(1,7) WIN\nLevel 2: E→(7,7), E→(4,7), E→(4,2) WIN\nLevel 3: E→(7,7), E→(1,7), E→(1,4), E→(5,4) WIN\n```\n\n### Spot checks of the trace\n\n- **Rotation inverse:** `toBoard` reverses the clockwise mapping. For Level 1, step 1, the rotated position (7,1) maps back to board position (7,7).\n- **Rotation direction:** on a 3×3 grid `abc/def/ghi`, `rotate(…,'E')` gives `gda/heb/ifc` and `rotate(…,'Q')` gives `cfi/beh/adg`.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- **All three solutions win when replayed:** yes by hand trace (2, 3 and 4 moves, each ending on H). This has not been confirmed by running the code.\n\n## Unverified items\n\n1. `checkSolutions(LEVELS)` has not been run in a JavaScript runtime.\n2. The breadth-first-search check that each level has no shorter solution (2, 3 and 4 moves) has not been run.\n3. These functions have not yet been wired into the three.js r128 game or its input lock and animation."
